                    {
                        at 2026-07-09T15:21:01.147391Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "LOG (LOG_OPENFST) backend REMOVED from the Rust port (2026-07-09), reversing the earlier \"finish LOG properly\" plan. Rationale: LOG was commented out of the C++ HFST types array (upstream-disabled, never shipped live); enabling it hit the log semiring's non-idempotency — common_divisor(One,One)=log-add(0,0)=-ln2!=One, so weighted determinization residuals drift unboundedly around cycles and the rule pipeline (minimize/subtract/intersect) is mathematically non-terminating. A rustfst determinize_unweighted spike fixed the drift but exposed exponential powerset blowup. Intractable and pointless. Deleted: crates/hfst/src/{log_weight_transducer.rs, convert_log_weight_transducer.rs}, examples/log_smoke.rs, LOG_OPENFST_TYPE from ImplementationType, LogFst/LogVectorFst/LogWeight from hfst-openfst, AnyTransducer::Log, all _log tests, openfst-log from every CLI format help+parser; reverted the rustfst determinize_unweighted/UnitCommonDivisor additions. KEPT: TransducerType::OPENFST_LOG_ wire-tag (recognizes+cleanly rejects legacy log .hfst files -> ERROR_TYPE). Spec/AKM effect: LOG is now out-of-scope like SFST/FOMA/XFSM — its spec .md files (Convert)LogWeightTransducer.md are retained as C++ upstream documentation; the rules stay covered by their source-impl (C++) annotations, so nplan_audit is clean (0 orphans / 0 stale / 0 new-uncovered). Left the LOG C++ files IN the source-impl set (not added to the exclude list) because excluding them drops coverage 81%->78% for those 103 C++-documented rules with no offsetting benefit; the current state is audit-clean. Verify: cargo build --workspace = 0 errors; cargo nextest run -p hfst = all pass (133/3-skip). Nextest now caps every test at 10s (.config/nextest.toml)."
                    }

