    {
        id perf-tropical-builder
        title "Optimize the tropical (OpenFST/rustfst-backed) FST builder — the main construction path. Profiled on a real lang-sma lexc compile (26 files -> 130,667-state / 222,603-arc lexicon, 5MB output): 7.7s wall (5.5s CPU, single-threaded) and 613MB peak RSS (>100x the output). dhat: 46.3M total allocations / 6.9M live at peak / 518MB at t-gmax, dominated by per-state Arc&lt;Vec&gt; transition storage and deep-copy FST conversions/clones. Children A/B/C are the ranked levers; harness is the cfg-gated dhat-heap CLI feature + /usr/bin/sample."
        children (
            {
                id conversion-churn
                title "(A) Kill redundant HfstBasicTransducer&lt;-&gt;StdVectorFst deep-copy conversions in the lexc-&gt;optimize pipeline. Each round-trip deep-copies the ~555K-state intermediate (hfst_basic_transducer_to_tropical_ofst + tropical_ofst_to_hfst_basic_transducer showed ~65MB and recur in both the sample and dhat profiles); rustfst TrsVec::clone is a deep Arc::new copy, so every conversion re-allocates all states. FIRST verify the conversion count around determinize AND minimize in optimize()/compile_lexical, then collapse to a single conversion / keep the FST in one representation across the ops. hfst-side, no rustfst change. Highest ranked — clearest win on both the 46M-alloc churn and the peak."
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:06:58.088519Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan_start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:30:55.030413Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "VERIFIED conversion count on the real lang-sma compile (.generated/lexicon.lexc, 133,744 lines -> 207,189-state / 382,315-arc lexicon). Env-gated counters in the two conversion fns showed 6,835 trop->basic + 5,249 basic->trop conversions per compile. Almost all (6,803) are tiny (<100 states); the COST is in ~25 big conversions (206K-999K states). Attribution via force_capture backtraces on states>=50k:\n - check_epsilon_cycles (the CHECK_EPSILON_CYCLES macro port) did 1,547 throwaway tropical->basic conversions, 7 of them on >=100k-state machines (2.27M states), purely to emit a diagnostic warning. Called from minimize 1523x, determinize 12x, are_equivalent 12x.\n - The remaining 36 big conversions are load-bearing facade round-trips: substitute_symbol_substitutions (6), prune_alphabet (6), new_from_basic (5, explicit in compile_lexical), harmonize_copy (5), harmonize_for_binary_op (4), new_from_transducer (2), compose_with_config (2), substitute_string (2), substitute_with_func (2), encode/decode_flag_diacritics (1 each).\n\nFIX SHIPPED: gate check_epsilon_cycles behind a cheap O(states+arcs) get_smallest_weight scan (no allocation) — a negative-weight epsilon cycle requires a negative-weight arc, so when the smallest weight is >= 0 the diagnostic can never fire and the throwaway deep-copy conversion is skipped. Faithful: warning fires in exactly the same cases (C++ built the basic transducer unconditionally but discarded it).\nRESULT: user CPU ~13.0s -> ~9.2s (~29%), byte-identical output (sha256 34f2debe...), all 1547 diagnostic conversions eliminated, 226 hfst tests pass, clippy-clean. Peak RSS unchanged (~990MB) as expected — the diagnostic conversions were transient.\n\nFINDING (why the rest can't just be collapsed): the remaining conversions feed downstream and their symbol re-canonicalization is load-bearing for byte-exact, C++-faithful output. check_epsilon_cycles was uniquely safe because its result is discarded. Eliminating the facade round-trips (native tropical prune_alphabet/insert/remove/harmonize/substitute operating on the symbol table in place, O(alphabet) not O(states)) is a bigger structural change that would perturb numbering -> must be validated by EQUIVALENCE not byte-identity. Split out as [[native-alphabet-ops]]."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:31:21.739203Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan_complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id rustfst-trsvec-csr
                title "(B) Replace rustfst's per-state TrsVec = Arc&lt;Vec&lt;Tr&gt;&gt; transition store with a flat/CSR representation (one arc array + per-state offsets, the OL-format idea). Today every VectorFst state = one Arc allocation for its transitions (555K states -&gt; 555K Arcs = the single biggest dhat category, ~110MB across default+clone sites), and &lt;TrsVec as Clone&gt;::clone does Arc::new (deep copy) so cloning an FST is O(states) allocations. This is the structural fix for the 46M-allocation churn. In the rustfst fork (git dep at necessary-nu/rustfst; editable via a [patch]). Bigger, higher-risk; benchmark against A first."
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T22:00:32.880758Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan_start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T23:06:38.433245Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "CSR REWRITE ATTEMPTED IN FULL AND PROVEN NON-VIABLE. Implemented the complete arena design in the fork clone (../rustfst): VectorFst gets one shared arc arena Arc<Vec<Tr>> + per-state (pos,ntrs) windows; VectorFstState stops owning arcs; get_trs returns a TrsConst window (cheap Arc::clone); mutation via an append/relocate/compact allocator with a `dead` hole counter + amortised maybe_compact; rewrote data_structure/mutable_fst/allocable_fst/iterators/fst/serializable_fst/parse_const/converters/trs_iter_mut + VectorFst PartialEq. It is CORRECT — all 133 rustfst non-openfst unit tests pass (determinize/minimize/optimize proptests incl.).\n\nBUT it is catastrophically slow on real workloads: one lang-sma compile went from ~10s to >3min (>18x, O(n^2)). Root cause (confirmed by /usr/bin/sample): acceptor_minimize -> merge_states holds a get_trs read handle (which Arc::clone's the single arena) WHILE calling add_tr; with one shared arena Arc, every add_tr's Arc::make_mut deep-copies the WHOLE arena (platform_memmove), so a single state merge is O(arena) -> O(n^2) overall. The per-state Arc<Vec<Tr>> is LOAD-BEARING: its make_mut COW is granular (only the mutated state's Vec clones, and only if that state is shared), which is exactly what rustfst's pervasive read-while-write algorithm code relies on. A single arena makes COW all-or-nothing, and cheap owned get_trs REQUIRES Arc sharing, so there is no arena variant that gives both cheap owned get_trs and granular COW. CSR is fundamentally incompatible with VectorFst as a general mutable FST.\n\nREVERTED the arena entirely. The one salvageable rustfst change is the shallow-clone bug-fix (TrsVec::clone: Arc::new(deep) -> Arc::clone(shallow)) — correct, byte-identical, 133 rustfst + 226 hfst tests pass — but NEUTRAL on the lang-sma builder (clones are mutated immediately; see conversion-churn/determinize-intermediate notes). RECOMMEND: retire the CSR node as non-viable; optionally land the shallow-clone bug-fix (fork push + Cargo.lock rev bump). The banked wins are A (conversion-churn) + C (determinize-intermediate): ~40% CPU, ~8% peak, byte-identical, no fork change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T23:19:37.072195Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "RETIRED as non-viable (closing the spike). Recommendation executed: (1) CSR arena fully reverted in the fork — no rustfst structural change lands; per-state Arc<Vec<Tr>> COW stays (load-bearing for read-while-write algorithms). (2) The one salvageable change, the shallow-clone bug-fix (TrsVec::clone: Arc::new deep-copy -> Arc::clone share), was pushed to the fork as c33ef8e8 and landed in hfst via Cargo.lock rev bump in commit 968fe55 — byte-identical on lang-sma (sha256 34f2debe...), 226 hfst + 133 rustfst tests pass. It is NEUTRAL on the lang-sma builder peak/CPU (clones are mutated immediately), kept as a correctness fix, not a perf win. The memory-reduction work continues via the sibling nodes minimize-consume-input (C2, next) and native-alphabet-ops (A2). Banked builder wins remain A (conversion-churn) + C (determinize-intermediate)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T23:19:40.508684Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan_complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id determinize-intermediate
                title "(C) Investigate + bound the determinize intermediate blowup: the lexicon determinizes to ~555K states before minimize collapses it to 130K, and determinize's lazy state table (find_id_from_ref -&gt; Arc per subset-state, ~27MB) + compute_trs vecs (~32MB) + the subset hashmap (~35MB single resize) are a large share of peak. Verify whether determinize_adaptive's budget/fallback (tropical_weight_transducer.rs) actually engages here, and whether an encode/minimize-earlier or streaming strategy shrinks the intermediate. Measure stage-by-stage state/arc counts first."
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:44:59.626432Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan_start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:52:41.702902Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "INVESTIGATION FINDING (overturns the node's premise): there is NO determinize intermediate blowup on the real lang-sma compile. Env-gated stage-size probe (HFST_STAGE_SIZES) over every minimize call showed input(post-rmeps) ~= determinized for ALL stages: 999,594 -> det 999,594; 211,358 -> 211,358; 212,475 -> 212,475; 206,897 -> 206,897; etc. The determinize step preserves state count (these are already-near-deterministic lexicons being re-optimized); it's Minimize that collapses them (999,594 -> 211,357). And determinize_adaptive's budget NEVER trips (0 fallbacks): budget = 256*input_states (e.g. 255M for the 999K machine) >> det_out. So the #435 safety net is correctly dormant here. The '555K->130K blowup' in the node title was from an older corpus/run; not reproduced.\n\nREAL peak driver: the ~1M-state (999,594) machine flowing through minimize. In the label-only path the input was cloned TWICE — once in minimize (let mut t = t.clone(), forced by the fn minimize(&self) trait signature) and again in determinize_adaptive (let mut labels_only = t.clone()) — so ~3 copies of the 1M-state machine were live at once (t + labels_only + growing det + determinize subset table).\n\nFIX SHIPPED: eliminate the second clone. Encode t in place with EncodeLabels and DeterminizeBounded(&*t, det) directly; on the rare budget overrun, Decode(t, label_mapper) restores the original before the weighted retry (result unchanged). RESULT: peak RSS ~990MB -> ~907MB (-83MB, ~8%), user CPU also down ~1s, byte-identical (sha256 34f2debe...), all 4 test_determinize_bounds tests pass (incl. the fallback test issue_435_att_minimize_terminates_and_is_correct), 226 hfst tests pass, clippy-clean.\n\nREMAINING (follow-up, not done here): the minimize-level `t = t.clone()` is still a full ~1M-state copy at peak. Removing it requires the AlgebraBackend::minimize/determinize trait methods to consume `self` (or take &mut) instead of &self, so the facade (self.fst = self.fst.minimize(...)) can hand over ownership via mem::take. That's a trait-signature change rippling to all backends (tropical/foma/OL) — bigger scope; would cut peak further. Same shape helps determinize()."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T21:52:45.388723Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan_complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id native-alphabet-ops
                title "(A2) Eliminate the load-bearing facade round-trips that deep-copy the whole graph to do alphabet-metadata / symbol-remapping work. Profiling (see conversion-churn) attributed the 36 big (206K-999K-state) conversions per lang-sma compile to: prune_alphabet (6), substitute_symbol_substitutions/substitute_string/substitute_with_func (10), harmonize_copy/harmonize_for_binary_op (9), insert/remove_from_alphabet, encode/decode_flag_diacritics. Each calls convert_to_basic_transducer() -> op -> convert_to_hfst_transducer(), i.e. two O(states) deep copies (rustfst TrsVec::clone is a per-state Arc::new). For the tropical backend the alphabet IS the input SymbolTable, so prune_alphabet/insert/remove could be an in-place O(alphabet) symbol-table edit with no graph copy. RISK: the round-trips re-canonicalize symbol numbering, which downstream stages rely on for byte-exact C++-faithful output — so native versions must be validated by EQUIVALENCE (same analyses/generations on lang-sma), not the byte-identical oracle. Successor port allows the divergence (see upstream-bugs-validation-successor). Biggest single event is a 999,594-state basic->trop; killing these also cuts peak (source+dest both live during a round-trip). Do AFTER benchmarking against lever B (rustfst-trsvec-csr), which reduces the per-conversion cost instead."
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-17T23:32:39.61823Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan_start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-18T00:02:34.945812Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "PEAK PREMISE OVERTURNED (same pattern as determinize-intermediate). Nativized the pure-metadata tropical alphabet ops — prune_alphabet, insert_to_alphabet (single+set), remove_from_alphabet — as in-place O(alphabet) SymbolTable edits (label-preserving via add_symbol_with_key), dispatched through new Backend trait methods (default round-trips; tropical overrides). This eliminates the 6 prune + insert/remove whole-graph deep-copy round-trips.\n\nMEASURED (interleaved C2-vs-A2 binaries, identical conditions, lang-sma lexc): peak RSS UNCHANGED at ~750 MiB (786M bytes) both; user CPU ~11.6s A2 vs ~12.3s C2 (neutral/noise); output BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256 34f2debe...); 226 hfst tests pass; fmt+clippy clean. Byte-identity held because the per-graph coder numbers the contiguous lexc intermediates as identity, so label-preserving in-place edits reproduce the round-trip's numbering exactly — no equivalence-only divergence needed.\n\nWHY NO PEAK WIN: the alphabet round-trips are TRANSIENT — they run before/between the determinize calls and are freed before determinize's subset construction (lazy state table + compute_trs vecs + subset hashmap, per node C) sets the high-water mark. The peak is determinize-owned; killing pre-peak transient copies reduces churn (allocation count) but not the high-water mark. The same holds for the remaining graph-relabel round-trips (substitute ~10, harmonize ~9, encode/decode flags): also transient/pre-peak, so nativizing them (in-place arc relabel) would cut churn but NOT peak either — and they mutate the graph (equivalence-only validation, higher risk). RECOMMEND: land the safe byte-identical metadata subset (done); do NOT pursue the graph-relabel remainder for peak (it can't move peak); revisit it only if a CPU/churn goal on other workloads justifies the risk. The node's peak target (below ~750 MiB) is not reachable via alphabet-op nativization."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-18T00:03:06.06518Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan_complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id minimize-consume-input
                title "(C2) Let minimize/determinize consume their input instead of cloning it. After determinize-intermediate removed the second (labels_only) clone, the FIRST clone remains: TropicalWeightTransducer::minimize/determinize start with `let mut t = t.clone()` because the AlgebraBackend trait methods take &self, yet the facade always does self.fst = self.fst.minimize(...) and drops the old fst immediately. On the profiled lang-sma compile that clone is a full ~1M-state (999,594) copy live at peak alongside det + the determinize subset table. Change AlgebraBackend::minimize/determinize (and the facade call sites) to hand over ownership — take self by value, or &mut self + std::mem::take — so no input clone is made. Ripples to all backends (tropical/foma/OL) implementing the trait. Byte-identity preserved (same algorithm, just no copy); measure peak. Would cut peak further than the ~907MB determinize-intermediate reached. Do alongside/after benchmarking lever B (rustfst-trsvec-csr), which attacks the same per-state Arc clone cost from the other side."
                log (
