                    {
                        at 2026-07-18T12:25:51.250868Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "CAUSAL REFRAME (2026-07-18, answers \"why still 1.4-1.6× slower than C++ on the xfst legs when outputs are byte-identical\"): byte-identity constrains WHAT is computed, not HOW FAST — the earlier \"algorithmic floor / byte-locked\" framing was wrong. The same chains run in OpenFST at ~100s vs our 164s (src/fst), and the divergence is in rustfst's lazy-FST framework, verified in code: (1) BOTH lazy caches (SimpleVecCache, SimpleHashMapCache: trs/start/final_weights fields) AND the state tables are std::sync::Mutex-wrapped — every get_trs/find_id/cache-fill pays lock+unlock in single-threaded static algorithms, millions of times per determinize; OpenFST CacheImpl is unsynchronized vectors. (2) the GENERIC lazy StateTable (lazy/state_table.rs BiHashMap<T, H = RandomState>) still hashes with SipHash — the determinize-specific table was FxHash-ified with a recorded ~5% win, compose et al. were not (sample evidence: sip frames under lazy StateTable::find_id + LazyFst::compute_bounded). (3) compute_bounded materializes cache-then-copy: computes each state into the Mutex-guarded cache then re-reads to build the output FST — pure overhead for one-shot static ops; plus get_trs returns an Arc clone (atomic refcount) per state visit. Frame evidence: `lazy` = 1223/~8000 (xfst sample), 7651/~10000 (errmodel determinize); likely contains the 680 unattributed compose frames. MAGNITUDE UNMEASURED — bounded above by the ~40% leg gap; measure per-change. Work order: (a) FxHash the generic StateTable (trivial, mirrors proven pattern); (b) de-Mutex the single-threaded path (RefCell/&mut cache variant — medium, design needed since LazyFst API is &self); (c) consider direct materialization bypassing the cache for static one-shot ops. All byte-safe (framework-only); validate with the standard oracle set + interleaved A/B per change."
                    }

