            {
                id pmatch-initial-symbols-dead
                title "INVESTIGATED, answer is DO NOT SHIP: wiring pmatch's dead initial-symbol optimisation up as ported makes a correct matcher silently drop matches, and the workload we care about gains nothing. Leave the code dead — matching is correct today BECAUSE it is dead. Re-scope to \"make the initial-symbol analysis sound\" only if a workload appears that emits the property"
                priority 2
                kind @perf
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:45:10.114629Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "DO NOT SHIP. Nothing committed; the wiring was built (~56 lines, faithful — verified to emit initial-symbols strings character-identical to the C++ oracle) and then reverted. Suite green at 340/340 with no change.\n\nWhere it fires it is worth a great deal — a 30-city/28-person gazetteer grammar gave 5.6x on a 3.9 MB input, 5.1x on 19.7 MB sparse, 1.46x on 25.3 MB dense, output identical in every case.\n\nTHE WORKLOAD WE ACTUALLY CARE ABOUT GAINS NOTHING. The real Giella lang-sma tokeniser-disamb-gt-desc.pmscript emits NO initial-symbols property at all — the alphabet is open, so the analysis bails, exactly as upstream does. Corroborated: 0 of 25 real-world C++-built .pmhfst archives on this machine carry the property. The compiled archive is byte-identical before/after (534,990,133 bytes). Cost on that same workload: 29.6-30.1s -> 34.0-35.2s, +14%. A node-cache fast path to claw it back measured 34.2 vs 34.2 — noise — and was reverted rather than shipped as unearned complexity.\n\nAND THE ANALYSIS IS UNSOUND, which is the decisive part. A 16-grammar sweep found two failing shapes:\n\n(1) Rust-only, from the AST associativity divergence now filed as nfst-pmatch-concat-associativity. `Define TOP LC({the }) [ {cat} | {dog} ] EndTag(a) ;` emits initial-symbols \" \" (a single space) where C++ emits \"cd\", and EVERY MATCH IS LOST. is_left_concatenation_with_context inspects only the top node's left child, so a left-folded Rust tree falls through to reading the REVERSED left context and returns its leading space. Same failure with a nullable prefix.\n\n(2) Shared with upstream — a genuine C++ soundness bug, filed under upstream-bug-report. `Define TOP [ [ LC({the }) {cat} ] | {dog} ] EndTag(a) ;` makes BOTH Rust-after and a C++-built archive emit \"d\" and silently drop the cat match. A context at the start of an alternation branch under-reports the allowed set. Upstream has been shipping this.\n\nSo the honest scoping is not \"wire up dead code\" but \"make the initial-symbol analysis sound\" — a design job needing the parser association reconciled and a correct nullability-aware analysis, with no upstream oracle to validate against since upstream is itself wrong. Gate any future attempt on a workload that actually emits the property; every Giella tokeniser we ship does not.\n\nBenchmark assets retained under the session scratchpad at pmi/ (bench/ gazetteer + inputs, adv/ the six adversarial grammars, sma/ the real tokeniser build)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:45:24.453062Z
