            {
                id foma-extract-paths-alignment
                title "backend_foma.rs:330 extract_paths_via_apply emits each path as a single StringPair of (whole input, whole output) instead of the per-symbol aligned column vector, so HfstTwoLevelPath::second.len() is always 1. Reached via extract_paths_cb/extract_paths_fd_cb and thus extract_paths/extract_paths_fd/extract_longest_paths/longest_path_size. Predicted: `print size` and longest-string-length under -f foma report 1 for every non-empty net. UNVERIFIED — confirm before fixing"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T09:08:08.388223Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "All three suspects CONFIRMED, but my predicted impact path for suspect 3 was REFUTED and the real one is worse. Verified independently here, not taken from the agent: longest-string-size on `a b c` now 3 under foma (was 1), matching tropical and C++; print-pairs on `[a:b c:d e] | [x y:0]` gives \"a:bc:de\" / \"xy:\" identically across foma, tropical and C++; flag diacritics now appear under --xfst=print-flags on foma (path ORDER differs from tropical, content matches — enumeration order is not specified); `test infinitely-ambiguous` gives a* -> 0 and [0:a]* -> 1 on foma, tropical and C++ alike, where foma previously answered 1 for a*.\n\nSuspect 1 root cause worth keeping: FomaTransducer::extract_paths DOES exist in C++ (FomaTransducer.cc:636) and does the depth-first line-table walk, but that file was excluded from the port's source-impl globs, so the stub was written blind against an oracle nobody could see. Exclusion from source-impl scope is therefore itself a risk signal — the excluded C++ still defines correct behaviour even when it is out of coverage scope.\n\nSuspect 3's fix is a deletion, not an implementation: Backend::is_infinitely_ambiguous's trait DEFAULT already delegates to to_basic()?.is_infinitely_ambiguous(), which is exactly what C++ HfstTransducer::is_infinitely_ambiguous does for every non-OL type (HfstTransducer.cc:932). The foma override was the bug. Whole-net cyclicity is not a conservative approximation of infinite ambiguity, it is a different question — infinite ambiguity needs an input-epsilon or flag cycle, so `a*` is cyclic and unambiguous. My \"conservative direction, low severity\" framing on the original node was wrong.\n\nREFUTED: no in-tree caller reaches FomaTransducer's LookupBackend::is_lookup_infinitely_ambiguous_*; flookup/lookup go via OlTransducer and xfst_compiler.rs:5299 is HfstBasicTransducer's method, not the backend's. The \"flookup truncates to infinite_cutoff 5\" scenario I described cannot occur.\n\nBonus fixed: extract_random_paths collapsed paths identically and IS reachable — xfst `print random-words` under foma printed \"ace:bde\" where tropical prints \"a:bc:de\". C++'s extract_random_paths is an empty stub, so no parity constraint.\n\nMerge note: the agent's `git commit -am` swept plan/.state.styx into its commit and the nplan merge driver could not parse the ancestor, so the merge conflicted there. Resolved --ours. Future agent briefs should say to stage explicitly rather than use -am."
                    }

