            {
                id xfst-set-minimal-unwired
                title "xfst_compiler.rs:1929 `set minimal ON/OFF` is parsed, echoed back to the user as `variable minimal = OFF` as if applied, then discarded via `let _ = text;`. Not a stub returning a consumed value, but the one place the port REPORTS SUCCESS for something it did not do. Either wire it to the minimization config or refuse the command"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:14:21.601104Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "WIRED, not refused. Merged nplan/xfst-set-minimal-unwired (96c458b). Verified here on the merged tree: `regex [a b c | x b c]` gives 4 states/4 arcs with `set minimal ON` and 7/6 with OFF, byte-identical to C++ hfst-xfst in both modes. 363/363 workspace, 270/270 foma.\n\nThe reason it looked unwireable is the interesting part, and it is the same defect one layer down. Setting the flag was trivial; what mattered is that all 16 optimize() call sites in the compiler used the DEFAULT EngineConfig — i.e. the C++ MAYBE_MINIMIZE sites were reading a hardcoded minimization: true. They now take the owned self.engine_config, which is where C++ read its can_minimize global. A flag reaching a field nobody reads is exactly the trap the brief warned about, and it was already present.\n\nSame shape found in two more variables while surveying all 35:\n- encode-weights reached engine_config, but engine_config had exactly ONE reader in the file (a compose call that ignores the field). Now has real consumers, and set_encode_weights is called to match the C++ global.\n- verbose was recorded and read nowhere, HERE AND UPSTREAM, despite self.verbose existing and gating print_transducer_info — C++ gates identically and simply never connected `set verbose` to it. Now wired.\n\nhopcroft-min got option (b), correctly: minimization_algorithm is documented inert at hfst_transducer.rs:3809-3812 because rustfst's Minimize does not branch on it, and that file was outside ownership. It now emits an ariadne warning that the build has no Brzozowski minimizer, with a note that the resulting network is identical either way.\n\nNine variables confirmed genuinely inert in BOTH implementations (char-encoding, copyright-owner, directory, quote-special, random-seed, recode-cp1252, recursive-define, sort-arcs, use-timer) — quote-special is a CLI flag of hfst-lookup/fst2strings, never an xfst behaviour. Collected in one INERT_VARIABLES table paired with the value that DOES describe the compiler, so `set` warns only when the request differs: `set directory OFF` is silent, `set char-encoding latin-1` is not. Four explanations that implied working features now say otherwise in `show variables`.\n\nBonus: the `no such variable` diagnostic advised 'show all', which is not a command in either implementation — the spelling is `show variables`. That was in the diagnostic the xfst-diagnostics node had just improved, so it was giving confidently wrong advice.\n\nAgent's note worth keeping: it observed the suite is 344 locally, not the 1598 in project memory, and inferred the fixture suite lives outside cargo nextest. Correct — that is the separate rustfst/oracle fixture harness."
                    }

