            {
                id silent-stub-lint-gate
                title "Close the ratchet on this bug class rather than re-sweeping. The tell is not TODO/unimplemented (those are loud) but a comment asserting a safety net that does not exist — \"callers needing the real X route through Y\" — beside a body returning self/empty/Ok(()). Every core-library instance was in backend_foma.rs, added after the idiom audits closed. Options: a review checklist for new backends, a test that every Backend trait method is exercised per backend, or a grep-based CI check for reassurance-comment shapes"
                priority 2
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T13:42:38.352729Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Built and merged (6ee0e94). 403/403 workspace, 290/290 foma, clippy and fmt clean.\n\nTHE TECHNIQUE IS THE THING WORTH REMEMBERING: a default cannot vary. The battery asks every query over fixtures whose truthful answers DIFFER, and every transformation over a fixture whose truthful result differs from its input. No constant, no self.clone(), no empty collection survives a pair of assertions like that — and it works without knowing which backend is under test, so a new backend inherits the whole battery for free. That is a much better ratchet than anything grep-shaped.\n\nCompile-time half: `classify` is a wildcard-free match over ImplementationType, so a new backend tag is an E0004 until its author places it, and a runtime test asserts every tag classified Battery actually ran, so the registry cannot claim coverage it does not have.\n\nSecond gate, trivial_body_census.rs: a frozen list of every trait-impl method whose whole body is a constant — exactly 7 workspace-wide, each with the reason it is honest. This reaches where behaviour cannot. FomaTransducer::push_weights returning self.clone() is CORRECT and unprovable: foma's line table has no weight field, so the right answer and a stub are observationally identical. A list entry with a reason is the only check such a method can get.\n\nProved load-bearing by reintroducing nine stubs of the swept shapes and reverting each; all nine fired at an assertion naming the defect, including restoring the number_of_states default while deleting foma's override — the case the compiler cannot catch.\n\nREJECTIONS, all with data, all correct:\n- Remove remaining defaults: nothing left. Every other value-returning default in the workspace does real work (the battery now asserts each takes effect), write_to_dir bails loudly, print_alphabet is the documented exception. PmatchObject's 20 defaults are faithful C++ base-class virtuals on the analysis 586ac73 deliberately left dead.\n- Reassurance-comment regex: the tell is not the comment, it is that the claim is FALSE, and you cannot grep for truth. Measured: 32 trivial-body functions tree-wide, and the CORRECT ones carry explanatory comments just as densely as the bug did.\n- clippy::missing_trait_methods: 532 hits in hfst alone — it fires on every legitimate default, which is the design of the trait.\n- clippy::allow_attributes + allow_attributes_without_reason WOULD have caught the #[allow(dead_code)] mask (because #[expect] self-destructs when the lint stops firing) but needs edits at ~110 sites. Worth its own node.\n\nTHREE REAL VIOLATIONS found and reported rather than fixed, each pinned by an expected-red test that fails the moment it is corrected — so they cannot rot in either direction. Filed as ol-header-flags-always-false (P0 — I verified the hang myself: 15,954 lines in 5s on a cyclic .hfstol where tropical refuses), foma-alphabet-set-ops, foma-first-vs-initial-symbols.\n\nHONEST LIMIT, and the agent named it precisely: a method returning a PLAUSIBLE BUT WRONG NON-CONSTANT value is invisible to both gates. foma's get_first_input_symbols is the demonstration — a real, non-empty, fixture-varying set that passes every assertion a semantics-blind battery can make. Only cross-backend differential testing against a backend known to be right catches that, and it requires someone to first suspect the two methods should differ."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T13:42:56.796308Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id hfst-info-bogus
                title "hfst-info reports fiction: it answers as upstream C++ HFST 3.17.1 and its feature checks are polarity-inverted. Two stacked bugs. (1) info.rs:29-36 simulates a config.h — PACKAGE_VERSION \"3.17.1\", HFST_LONGVERSION 300170001, HAVE_SFST true — so `info` prints \"HFST version: 3.17.1\" and \"SFST supported\" for a Divvun HFST 0.1.0 build with SFST out of scope, and `-a 3.17.0` exits 0 on a false claim. (2) info.rs:275/285/295 test `if HAVE_FOMA`/`HAVE_XFSM`/`HAVE_OPENFST` where the SFST branch correctly tests `!HAVE_SFST`, so -f foma and -f openfst EXIT 1 for backends that ARE supported while -f sfst exits 0 for one that is not. The inversion is faithfully ported from an upstream C++ bug (hfst-info.cc:237 `#if HAVE_FOMA` guarding \"Required foma support not present\") — fix it, per the successor reframe, rather than preserving it. Report upstream. hfst-info is a build-time detection tool and giella-core configure.ac:116 probes for it, so wrong answers steer configure"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T08:51:26.889788Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed on nplan/hfst-info-bogus (ba64dcb), merged to main as ae4911b. Verified independently, not taken from the agent's report: -f sfst 1, -f foma 0, -f openfst 0, -f xfsm 1, -a 3.17.0 1, -e 3.17.1 1, -a 0.1.0 0, -m 0.0.1 1, -m 9.9.9 0. 314/314 nextest, clippy and fmt clean.\n\nVersion gating now compares against this crate's version in the encoding parse_version_string already uses (0.1.0 -> 10000, from CARGO_PKG_VERSION_* at compile time). A build script asking \"is this HFST >= 3.17\" gets a truthful no through the interface it already speaks, and the refusal names what this is rather than being mysterious.\n\nStructural point worth keeping: the -f gate and the informational listing now read ONE FEATURES table instead of two independently-maintained ladders. Two ladders disagreeing is what produced the inversion. foma now tracks cfg!(feature = \"foma\") rather than a hardcoded true, and negatives are printed explicitly so an absent line is not ambiguous between \"unsupported\" and \"old build\".\n\nTHIRD upstream bug found en route and fixed: --max-version used the --atleast-version comparison (HFST_LONGVERSION < max_version instead of >), so it accepted exactly the builds it was meant to reject. Confirmed still present upstream at hfst-cpp/src/tools/src/hfst-info.cc:214-217. Worth including in the upstream report alongside the -f polarity inversion.\n\nLeft for elsewhere: hfst_set_program_name(&argv0, \"0.1\", \"HfstInfo\") still passes a stale upstream per-tool version literal, as do all ~63 tools; info no longer prints it but the field survives. And -f icu reports ICU supported unconditionally, with no cargo feature behind it as foma has."
                    }

