            {
                id naive-tokenizer-harmonizer-alphabet
                title "make_naive_tokenizer (pmatch_tokenize.rs:1431) builds its harmonizer with hfst_transducer_to_hfst_ol(dictionary) WITHOUT the \"harmonizer_alphabet\" option that convert_ol_transducer.rs:180-205 documents as the fix for exactly this index-table under-padding (upstream hfst#354). That is why `hfst tokenize &lt;plain .hfstol&gt;` reached the pmatch table probe at all. Input matching is unaffected — input symbols are precisely the padded ones — so the runtime bounds fix in panic-on-malformed-input is sufficient for correctness, but the option is arguably missing here and its absence is what routed a plain transducer into the pmatch path. Found by the panics agent, outside its file ownership"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:14:48.05473Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "VERDICT: NO CHANGE. make_naive_tokenizer is correct as-is; the test-only commit (66fde4c) pins the conclusion so the question is not reopened from scratch. Merged.\n\nWhat harmonizer_alphabet actually does: in get_states_and_symbols it reclassifies every alphabet symbol appearing in NO transition as an input symbol, which permutes numbering (real input symbols get pushed into the alphabet tail), grows input_symbol_count to the whole alphabet, and pads the index table by that many blanks.\n\nThat is right for write_archive, which harmonizes against a transition-LESS alphabet holder — without the option every symbol is output-only, input_symbol_count is 1, and the numbering imposed on archive members carries no input structure. make_naive_tokenizer is the opposite case: the harmonizer IS the dictionary, a real transducer whose transitions already say which symbols are inputs.\n\nMeasured cost, A/B on the real code path: dictionary index table 13 -> 445 (34x); on a larger synthetic 55 -> 2059 (37x). Benefit measured at zero — tokenize output byte-identical with and without; an over-range probe answers exactly like the blank entry longer padding would have provided; the pmatch runtime never consults input_symbol_count (PmatchContainer builds its Encoder over orig_symbol_count); and the tables are never serialized here.\n\nThe decisive argument is one I had not considered: it could not be avoided anyway. EVERY plain .hfstol ever written pads for its input symbols only, and hfst tokenize must accept those files as dictionaries. So the fix genuinely belongs in the runtime bounds check that already landed under panic-on-malformed-input, not at this call site. C++ hfst-tokenize.cc:140-227 passes the dictionary with no options — same shape.\n\nSEPARATE FINDING, now filed as tokenize-drops-unmatched: the `others` fallback never fires, so unmatched words are silently dropped. I verified against C++ on the same fixture and input — it drops them too, so the port is faithful and this is upstream behaviour, not a defect. Filed as a divergence candidate rather than a bug."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T11:15:05.78157Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id getopt-clustered-shorts
                title "Clustered short options are unsupported across the WHOLE CLI: hfst_getopt.rs treats an option as short only when `name.chars().count() == 1` and never splits a cluster, so `-wq` is rejected with \"Invalid option\" while `-w -q` works. C++ accepts both. Verified against the oracle on hfst-optimized-lookup (-wq is the ONE differing case out of 12 flag spellings tested; every other matches byte-for-byte) and reported also for hfst-fst2strings -qn. This is a flag-compatibility contract with Giella build scripts and hand-typed invocations, not a diagnostic nicety — any script using a cluster fails. Cross-cutting: one fix in hfst_getopt.rs repairs every tool. Found by the dead-code-allow-census agent, outside its file ownership"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T13:32:01.470245Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed. Merged nplan/getopt-clustered-shorts (42cd441). 389/389, clippy and fmt clean. Verified here: `-wq` on hfst-optimized-lookup now matches C++ byte-for-byte; `-wqu` and `-w -q -u` agree; `-wn 2`, `-wn2`, `-wn` (missing arg) and bare `-` as stdin all behave as the oracle does.\n\nThe fix keeps the single-table design as instructed — letters resolve through the same longopts scan a lone `-f` uses, so no second table exists to drift. Two details were load-bearing and are worth remembering: the pending-cluster check must run BEFORE the end-of-argv test or a trailing cluster loses its last letter to finish(), and the scan runs over the dash-stripped token rather than the =-split name, which is what preserved the attached-argument case (-Wall, -n2, -pa=b) — that separate GNU fallback is now deleted because the cluster path subsumes it.\n\nStrongest evidence in the report is not the agreements but the disagreements: of 67 cluster-vs-separated groupings across 8 tools, both implementations agree 67/67 — INCLUDING three pairings the agent initially got wrong, where C++ and Rust rejected them identically (-en1 is `-e n1` not `-e -n 1`; -Sq is `-S=q`; -exq is `-e xq`). Agreement on cases where the expected answer was wrong is much better evidence than agreement on easy ones.\n\nDeliberately not implemented, all defensible: getopt's own opterr diagnostics (\"invalid option -- Z\"), which this port has never emitted — that is the sole content of the one edge case where we still differ from C++; GNU's literal `=` handling for a single-letter short (-n=2), where this port is leniently a superset so nothing that works in C++ breaks, and the GNU rule DOES hold inside a cluster; and `-verbose` as a long name behind one dash, which still resolves as a long name because that lookup runs first.\n\nTHREE FINDINGS OUTSIDE SCOPE, from a 131-invocation oracle diff (109 match; of the 22 differences, 19 were message text or stream routing). Filed as invert-symbol-table-duplication and ol-fast-mode-silent. The invert one I verified myself: 393 bytes vs C++'s 560 on [a:b c:d], hfst-compare EQUIVALENT, cause is C++ writing the symbol table twice. giella-core invokes hfst-invert, so it bears on byte-parity claims."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T13:32:17.359197Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
