            {
                id tokenize-drops-unmatched
                title "hfst tokenize SILENTLY DROPS unmatched input: with a plain .hfstol dictionary, `dogs cot cats` yields \"dogs\\ncats\" — the word `cot` vanishes, even though every letter is in the dictionary's alphabet. With -a the run is emitted as one blob including surrounding spaces. NOT a port defect: C++ hfst-tokenize does exactly the same on the same fixture and input (verified). The naive tokenizer's `others` fallback (make_exc_list(word_boundary)+, weighted f32::MAX) never fires; cause is downstream in the pmatch runtime / exclusion-list handling in crates/hfst/src/pmatch.rs, and make_naive_tokenizer faithfully mirrors the C++. So this is a DIVERGENCE CANDIDATE under [dec:hfst:independent-fork], not a bug to fix silently: a tokenizer that discards input is arguably wrong, but tokenizer output IS a contract with Giella pipelines, so changing it needs an explicit call. Decide, do not drift. Found by the naive-tokenizer agent"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
            }

            {
                id nfst-pmatch-concat-associativity
                title "nfst-pmatch folds juxtaposition LEFT where C++ bison shift-resolves RIGHT, so the AST shape diverges even though the language does not. Verified: nfst-pmatch parser.rs parse_expression7 builds `left = Concat(left, right)` in a loop; the C++ rule `EXPRESSION7: EXPRESSION7 EXPRESSION7` (pmatch_parse.yy:333) is ambiguous and bison resolves by shifting. For `A B C` Rust yields Concat(Concat(A,B),C), C++ Concat(A,Concat(B,C)). Concatenation is associative so matching and pmatch2fst output are UNAFFECTED — this is latent, not a live bug. It bites any analysis that pattern-matches on tree shape: it is precisely why the ported is_left_concatenation_with_context misreads a Rust tree, inspecting only the top node's left child and falling through to the reversed left context. Fix is in the sibling repo (necessary-nu/nfst) and needs a release; alternatively any shape-based analysis must handle both foldings. Decide which before writing another such analysis"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
            }

            {
                id upstream-bug-report
                title "Report the upstream C++ HFST bugs this sweep confirmed still present in 3.17.1, with reproductions. FIVE so far: (1) hfst-info feature checks are polarity-inverted — hfst-info.cc:237 guards \"Required foma support not present\" with `#if HAVE_FOMA`, so -f foma and -f openfst FAIL on builds that have them; (2) hfst-info --max-version uses the --atleast-version comparison (hfst-info.cc:214-217), accepting exactly the builds it should reject; (3) pmatch initial-symbol analysis is UNSOUND for a context at the start of an alternation branch — `Define TOP [ [ LC({the }) {cat} ] | {dog} ] EndTag(a) ;` emits initial-symbols \"d\" and SILENTLY DROPS the cat match, reproduced with a C++-built archive; (4) unicodeCheck_ hardcodes the first two code points and appends a literal \"...\" so its \"add X to Multichar_Symbols\" advice is wrong for any 3+ code point grapheme; (5) hfst-invert writes the symbol table TWICE, producing a 560-byte file where 393 suffices — verified redundant, since our single-table file reads back in C++ with identical paths and alphabet. Each was found by fixing our port and checking whether upstream shares it. Reproductions are in this session's history"
                priority 2
                kind @docs
            }

            {
                id invert-symbol-table-duplication
                title "RESOLVED by operator decision: keep our smaller artifact, C++'s duplicate symbol table is an upstream bug. hfst invert emits 393 bytes where C++ emits 560 on [a:b c:d], because C++ writes the symbol table TWICE. Verified our file loses nothing — C++ reads it back with identical paths (b:ad:c) and an identical alphabet, and hfst-compare says equivalent. HFST's container carries one sigma, so the second copy is pure redundancy. Consequence to act on: any \"byte-identical to the C++ CI reference\" claim for a lang build that invokes hfst-invert is now knowingly false and must be restated as behavioural equivalence"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-12T13:37:09.069702Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator decision: we keep our artifact; C++'s duplicate symbol table is an upstream bug. No code change.\n\nVerified before recording, because \"better artifact\" only holds if ours is complete: C++ hfst-fst2strings reads OUR inverted file and prints b:ad:c, identical to what we print from ITS file; hfst-dump-alphabets output is identical between the two; hfst-compare says equivalent. The HFST container carries a single sigma, so the second table C++ writes is pure redundancy — nothing is lost by omitting it, and interop is unaffected in both directions.\n\nUnder [dec:hfst:independent-fork] this is the straightforward case: a smaller file that reads correctly everywhere beats byte-parity with a redundant one, and formats stay a contract only where the contract carries information. It does not here.\n\nCONSEQUENCE THAT NEEDS ACTING ON, not just noting. giella-core invokes hfst-invert, so any lang build passing through invert now produces files that are byte-different from a C++-produced reference BY DESIGN. The lang-sma and lang-sme results recorded as \"byte-identical to the C++ CI reference\" predate this finding and were measured on a toolchain that had not yet been compared this way. Those claims must be restated as behavioural equivalence — hfst-compare equivalent, same analyses — rather than byte identity, or re-measured excluding invert-derived artifacts. This is a knowing divergence now, so a stale byte-identity claim would be a false claim rather than an unverified one.\n\nAdded to upstream-bug-report as the fifth confirmed-live C++ bug."
                    }

