            {
                id ol-fast-mode-silent
                title "`hfst optimized-lookup -f` (fast mode) prints NOTHING where C++ echoes the input words; and `hfst lookup -v` diverges deeper in its verbose output though the first lines match. Both reproduce on UNCLUSTERED spellings, so neither is a getopt problem. Found by the getopt-clustered-shorts agent in a 131-invocation C++-vs-Rust diff whose other 22 differences were all message-text or stream-routing noise — these two are payload. Verify each against the oracle and decide: silent fast mode looks like a straightforward port gap rather than a deliberate divergence"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:01:44.484484Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:07:13.675531Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Both halves resolved; the fast-mode half was a real port gap, the lookup -v half was noise.\n\nFast mode. The node's guess (\"a straightforward port gap\") was right, and the gap was structural. Fast mode in C++ is a STREAMING path, not a quieter one: Transducer::note_analysis has an `if (beFast)` branch that writes each analysis straight to stdout as it is found, and printAnalyses is then wrapped in `if (!beFast)` precisely because the work is already done. The port took the printAnalyses half (return early under be_fast) and not the note_analysis half, so nothing was printed at all. Only the unweighted plain variant is affected — Transducer::note_analysis is the sole one with a beFast branch and the sole one whose printAnalyses is guarded — which is why an OLW transducer looked fine under -f and the committed unweighted fixture printed nothing.\n\nFixing that exposed a second fold. C++ has two distinct paths to a +? record: tokenization failure in the main loop (NOT beFast-guarded, skips the lookup entirely) and no-analyses in printAnalyses (beFast-guarded). The port had only the second, so a word the alphabet cannot even spell was treated identically to one that spells fine and is not in the language. Invisible outside fast mode, where both print. Verified against the oracle: `-f` on `xyz` prints the +? record, `-f` on `act` prints nothing.\n\nTelling them apart needed a strict tokenizer probe. Transducer::initialize_input is the wrong instrument: it ADOPTS an unrecognized symbol into the alphabet and carries on, failing only on malformed UTF-8, where C++'s find_next_key loop stops at the first unknown. Added Transducer::can_tokenize (non-mutating, uses the existing encoder's find_key) and exposed it on the OL lookup facade. In the tool it is asked of the whole stream — any member that can tokenize is enough, since the members are looked up as a union.\n\nA/B'd against C++ 3.17.1 across -f, -e, -e -f and no flags: every line agrees except the deliberate blank-line divergence.\n\nlookup -v was noise, contrary to the node's \"diverges deeper in its verbose output\". Two differences, both cosmetic: upstream renders the format strings with the archaic backtick-apostrophe quoting where we use plain quotes, and its warning is prefixed with argv[0] as a full path where ours names the multiplexer subcommand. Neither is payload; both are the message-text class the node's author had already excluded.\n\nKept and now spec'd: upstream prints a +? record followed by THREE blank lines (record terminator, then two more from an unconditional second statement) where an analysed word gets one. We emit one uniformly. Recorded as PORT DIVERGENCE on run-transducer-fn and pinned by an_unknown_word_terminates_like_any_other, which puts the unknown word mid-stream — the golden fixture has it last, where the difference cannot be seen.\n\nLeft alone deliberately: the variant selection is a 4-way (weighted, unique) match, where C++ picks among 8 classes on (flags, weighted, unique). Variant::Fd and Variant::WFd are therefore unreachable. Flag diacritics are handled inside the library engine (lookup_fd_string) rather than by variant, so this is probably vestigial rather than broken, but it is unexamined — filed as ol-variant-fd-unreachable."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:07:39.017992Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ol-header-flags-always-false
                title "is_cyclic and is_infinitely_ambiguous are constant false for the WHOLE OL family, and it hangs a live tool. Transducer::is_cyclic probes HeaderFlag::Cyclic and is_infinitely_ambiguous probes Has_input_epsilon_cycles, but TransducerHeader::set_flag has ZERO callers in the workspace and both new_weighted (transducer.rs:568) and new_sizes (:596) hardcode all seven property flags false — only a header read off disk (:618) can set them. So every in-memory-built OLW/THFST transducer answers false whatever it contains. Worse than an inherited default: it OVERRIDES a trait default at backend.rs:296 that would compute the answer from the graph, under a comment reading \"the OL backends answer directly\". MEASURED CONSEQUENCE: `hfst fst2strings` on a cyclic .hfstol runs UNBOUNDED — 15,954 lines in 5s, longest 31,824 chars and growing — where the same net as tropical refuses immediately with \"Transducer is cyclic. Use one or more of these options\". Live readers: hfst_transducer.rs:808/824, xfst_compiler.rs:6372/6431/6593, fst2strings.rs:622/738. deterministic/input_deterministic/minimized are hardcoded TRUE by the same constructors, same shape, no live readers today. NOT fixed by the gate agent because the obvious repair turns an O(1) header read into an O(states) walk on the pmatch/lookup hot path — that trade is an operator call. Pinned by an expected-red test"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T14:52:27.636942Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FIXED. The agent stalled mid-verification without committing, so I assessed its uncommitted work, completed the outstanding checks and committed it myself. 416/416 workspace (up from 403), 303/303 foma, clippy and fmt clean, cargo check --no-default-features clean.\n\nP0 verified gone by hand: `fst2strings` on a cyclic .hfstol now refuses with \"Transducer is cyclic. Use one or more of these options: -n, -N, -r, -l, -L, -c\" and produces ZERO lines in 5s, where it previously emitted 15,954 with the longest line at 31,824 chars and growing. `a*` and `[0:a]*` both trip the guard; `[a b]` correctly does not.\n\nMY BRIEF'S PREMISE ABOUT THE COST WAS RIGHT AND THE NODE'S WAS WRONG: is_cyclic has no caller in the OL lookup runtime or in pmatch.rs — every one is a path-extraction guard or an interactive xfst test, on operations that already traverse the graph. The \"O(1) header read becomes an O(states) walk on the hot path\" objection that deferred this had no hot path behind it.\n\nDESIGN CHOICE, and it is the right one: always compute, never trust the flag. That makes the write-path stamping I had asked for MOOT rather than incomplete — convert_ol_transducer.rs is deliberately untouched. I nearly recorded that file as unfinished work; it is not. Nothing reads the flags now, and an unreliable flag is worse than a cheap walk since third-party files may carry the same wrong values this port was writing. THFST carries no header at all, so no lie is possible there either.\n\nVerified no consumer is misled, which was the open question: C++ hfst-fst2strings reads a cyclic .hfstol written by us and refuses correctly.\n\nThe semantic distinction is pinned by tests rather than assumed: infinite ambiguity is strictly narrower than cyclicity — it needs an INPUT-EPSILON cycle, so a* is cyclic and finitely ambiguous while [0:a]* is both. Named tests star_is_cyclic_but_finitely_ambiguous and epsilon_star_is_cyclic_and_infinitely_ambiguous, 14 in the new ol_graph_properties.rs.\n\nThe ratchet's expected-red assertion in backend_answers.rs was updated to assert the fixed behaviour rather than deleted, as instructed — so the gate still covers this method.\n\nSTILL OPEN from the original finding: deterministic / input_deterministic / minimized are hardcoded TRUE by the same constructors. No live readers today, which is exactly where has_weights sat before the previous sweep, and true is the more dangerous direction — a false positive on \"already minimized\" silently skips an operation where a false negative merely costs work."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T14:52:56.645145Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id foma-alphabet-set-ops
                title "FomaTransducer::add_symbols_to_alphabet returns Ok(()) having added nothing: foma overrides insert_to_alphabet but INHERITS the round-trip default for the set form, and from_basic (backend_foma.rs:141) builds the sigma purely by interning each arc's symbols — it never reads the interchange net's alphabet — so any symbol on no arc is dropped. remove_from_alphabet is the same defect with the opposite sign: it appears to work only because the round trip drops arc-less symbols regardless, and it cannot touch a symbol still on an arc. Two paths on the same backend disagree about what the alphabet is. Consumer to check when fixing: hfst_xerox_rules strips its temporary markers via remove_from_alphabet_symbol/_set on every replace-rule compile. Found by the ratchet battery; pinned by an expected-red test"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:02:45.98179Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Root cause fixed in from_basic, not just the two methods. FomaTransducer::from_basic now declares every remaining member of the interchange alphabet in the sigma (sigma_add per missing non-reserved symbol, then sigma_sort) after fsm_construct_done. That repairs the round trip for every Backend default routed through it, not only the alphabet edits: prune_alphabet (which previously behaved as force=true regardless of the flag, because the round trip dropped arc-less symbols whether or not unknown/identity were on arcs), encode_flag_diacritics / decode_flag_diacritics (both explicitly copy the alphabet into the rebuilt basic net, so arc-less flag entries vanished), and the facade's generic substitution. to_hfst_ol and is_infinitely_ambiguous only read to_basic, so they were never lossy themselves.\n\nsigma_sort is not optional after a sigma_add: fsm_merge_sigma walks two sigmas as one ordered merge keyed on strcmp, so an unsorted sigma silently mismaps arcs. The pre-existing insert_to_alphabet override did a bare sigma_add with neither a sort nor a duplicate check, violating foma's own documented precondition on sigma_add; both are now handled by the shared sigma_declare helper. I could not construct a case where the unsorted sigma visibly corrupted a result (the merge's two-pointer walk mostly self-heals), so this is defensive, not a demonstrated corruption.\n\nRemoval semantics decided deliberately: alphabet-only, never touches the graph. A foma arc addresses its symbol by sigma NUMBER, so dropping an entry an arc still carries leaves that arc pointing into a hole which the following sigma_sort fills with a neighbouring symbol — a silent relabel. The other two readings are worse (deleting the arcs changes the language; erroring breaks callers that strip a marker set wholesale). So an in-use symbol keeps its entry and every entry for that symbol which no arc uses is dropped. This is a deliberate divergence from the SymbolTable backends, which will unname a live arc label on request. It never bites in practice: every caller removes a symbol it has just substituted off the graph, which is also foma's own idiom (sigma_remove + sigma_sort, as in rewrite.rs / derived.rs).\n\nNo change to the foma crate was needed: sigma_add / sigma_find / sigma_sort are public and sufficient."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:02:50.648365Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:16:19.396105Z
