            {
                id foma-first-vs-initial-symbols
                title "foma answers get_first_input_symbols and get_initial_input_symbols with ONE shared helper, under a comment at backend_foma.rs:471 claiming \"foma draws no distinction between them\" — the same reassurance shape, asserting an equivalence the contract does not have. Tropical's are different walks: get_initial_input_symbols descends THROUGH epsilon and flag arcs and stops at the first real symbol; get_first_input_symbols walks the whole net. Measured on the acceptor `abc`: tropical gives initial=[a] first=[a,b,c], foma gives initial=[a] first=[a]. Currently LATENT — summarize is the only live caller and its foma arm returns None — which is exactly the state has_weights was in before the previous sweep. NOT caught by either new gate: the value is real, non-empty and varies correctly across fixtures, so it passes every assertion a semantics-blind battery can make. This is the demonstration case for what the ratchet cannot catch"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
            }

            {
                id ol-header-flags-hardcoded-true
                title "deterministic / input_deterministic / minimized are hardcoded TRUE by TransducerHeader::new_weighted and new_sizes, the same constructors whose false flags caused the ol-header-flags-always-false hang. No live reader today — which is precisely where has_weights sat before the previous sweep found it. TRUE is the more dangerous direction than false: a false positive on \"already minimized\" silently SKIPS an operation, where a false negative merely costs work. Same fix shape as the cyclic case: either compute from the graph (ol_walk exists) or delete the fields if nothing should ever consult them. Check has_epsilon_epsilon_transitions and has_input_epsilon_transitions in the same pass"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T23:50:10.754732Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Premise was stale on the main charge, and the real defect was next to it.\n\nStale part: the P0 fix (741b9d9) had already dealt with the hardcoded trues. new_sizes leaves every property flag false (\"nothing claimed\"), and Transducer::write derives the decidable flags from the graph via header_with_graph_properties before emitting. undecidable_properties_are_not_claimed already round-trips a non-minimal input-nondeterministic net through a written file and asserts Deterministic / Input_deterministic / Minimized all read false. Confirmed by grep that those three have no reader anywhere outside the header printer.\n\nnew_weighted's trues are honest and stay: that constructor builds the one-state no-arc header, and it is only ever paired with empty tables (Transducer::new_empty and create_empty_transducer). A 1-state 0-arc net is deterministic, input-deterministic, minimized and acyclic.\n\nThe real defect, found by the \"check the epsilon flags in the same pass\" instruction: the two epsilon flags read \"input epsilon\" differently. has_input_epsilon_cycles used consumes_no_input (epsilon OR flag diacritic, the reading the lookup engine and is_infinitely_ambiguous take); has_input_epsilon_transitions used a literal arc.input == 0. A flag-only cycle therefore wrote a header claiming an input-epsilon CYCLE while denying it had any input-epsilon TRANSITION — a graph that cannot exist. Pinned expected-red first (the_header_reads_flags_as_the_engine_does), then made the transition flag use consumes_no_input. has_epsilon_epsilon_transitions stays strict symbol-zero on both tapes and no longer hangs off the input flag: a flag is written to the output tape, and stripping it is the lookup formatter's business, not a property of the graph.\n\nSeparately, cargo check --no-default-features did not build at all on main (22 errors) and had nothing to do with this node. The workspace declared hfst = { path = \"crates/hfst\" } with defaults on, so hfst-cli built against a library that still had AnyTransducer::Foma while --no-default-features gated away the CLI match arms for it. Fixed at the workspace declaration (default-features = false) so a consumer's own backend features are the only thing that turn a backend on; hfst-cli's default = [\"foma\"] keeps ordinary builds identical. Filed no-default-features-ungated for the missing gate."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T23:50:30.334693Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id no-default-features-ungated
                title "cargo check --no-default-features was broken on main for an unknown span and nothing noticed: hfst-cli built against an hfst that still carried AnyTransducer::Foma while its own match arms were gated away, 22 E0004 errors. Root cause fixed (workspace hfst dep now default-features = false), but the gap that let it rot is unfixed — there is no CI in this repo at all (no .github), and the nplan rust platform block runs only cargo fmt --check, gated on crates/**/*.rs so a Cargo.toml-only change would not trip it anyway. Needs a feature-combination build gate somewhere that actually runs. Commit checks are budgeted at 60s and a feature-flip forces a full rebuild, so the commit hook is likely the wrong home for it"
                priority 2
                kind @build
            }

            {
                id foma-generic-path-parity-tests
                title "Four sites in hfst_transducer.rs carried comments saying foma was compiled out, in a tree where foma is a live validated backend: harmonize_copy's no-harmonization arm, its flag-diacritic pre-insertion block, extract_random_paths/n_best, and compose_intersect. Each documented a C++ arm dropped on the grounds the backend did not exist. All four verified correct through the generic path by CLI A/B (compose-intersect on .foma inputs gives the same result as tropical; union of foma nets with disjoint alphabets, unknowns, and a flag diacritic agrees path-for-path, 16 and 3 paths) and the prose is corrected, but only the prose — nothing gates it. Add the cross-backend parity cases to foma_backend.rs next to assert_binary_parity, which already has fac_foma/fac_trop: compose_intersect is uncovered there, and so are the disjoint-alphabet and flag-carrying harmonization inputs. Deferred behind the alphabet-set-ops work to avoid two agents editing the same test file"
                priority 2
                kind @test
            }

            {
                id ol-variant-fd-unreachable
                title "optimized_lookup.rs picks its display Variant from a 2-tuple (weighted, unique), giving 4 outcomes, where C++ setup picks among 8 classes on 3 booleans (has-flag-diacritics, weighted, unique). Variant::Fd, Variant::WFd and Variant::WFdUniq are consequently constructed nowhere, though every match arm still handles them. Most likely vestigial rather than broken: flag diacritics are resolved inside the library engine via lookup_fd_string, so the tool may not need a per-flag variant at all. But that is a hypothesis, not a finding — nobody has checked whether the Fd classes differ from their non-Fd counterparts in any way that survives that difference. Decide it: either prove the distinction is subsumed and delete the dead variants, or restore the third selector. Found while fixing ol-fast-mode-silent"
                priority 2
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:34:22.206343Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Resolved: the distinction IS subsumed. The three unreachable variants are deleted. But the A/B that proved it also found a live output bug next door, now fixed, plus a third divergence recorded for a separate node.\n\nSOURCE ANSWER, per pair. (1) Transducer vs TransducerFd: TransducerFd declares neither printAnalyses nor note_analysis (hfst-optimized-lookup.h:771-791) and inherits both from Transducer verbatim. Identical. (2) TransducerW vs TransducerWFd: same shape (h:1234-1256) — no printAnalyses, no note_analysis, inherits TransducerW. Identical. (3) TransducerUniq vs TransducerFdUniq: both declare their own private DisplaySet plus note_analysis and printAnalyses. The two note_analysis bodies (cc:1152 and cc:1166) are character-identical. The two printAnalyses bodies (cc:1325 and cc:1384) are identical on every non-WINDOWS path; they differ only in one stray blank line in the source. (4) TransducerWUniq vs TransducerWFdUniq: note_analysis bodies (cc:1842, cc:1860) character-identical. printAnalyses (cc:1961, cc:2049) differ only in while-loop vs for-loop syntax and in a WINDOWS-only bug where WFdUniq passes std::string to hfst_fprintf_console's %s. No POSIX difference.\n\nSo yes to question 3: the Fd classes differ from their non-Fd counterparts ONLY in try_epsilon_transitions plus PushState, i.e. flag-diacritic filtering during traversal. Eight classes, four display behaviours. The library engine behind lookup_fd_string does that filtering (crates/hfst/src/transducer.rs:3467-3491, flag_state.apply_operation gating the recursion), so there is no traversal to select either.\n\nRust side: the print_analyses Variant::WFd arm was a byte-for-byte copy of the Variant::WPlain arm, which is the same fact showing up in the port.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL A/B. Fixtures built with the C++ toolchain (hfst-regexp2fst | hfst-fst2fst -w / -O) so both tools read the identical bytes: a flag-bearing analyser in unweighted and weighted OL, a weighted flag variant with duplicate analyses and distinct weights, and a no-flag control of each. That the C++ side really takes the Fd branch is not assumed: a non-Fd class returns from try_epsilon_transitions at the first non-epsilon transition, so every flag-guarded path would vanish; C++ prints them, so state size is non-zero and the Fd class was chosen.\n\n6 fixtures x 21 option sets (plain, -u, -w, -u -w, -f, -e, -n, -b, -x and combinations), compared as od -c. FIRST RUN: 68 of 102 diverged — every single flag-bearing run, none of the no-flag ones. Not the Fd display though: our tool was printing the flag diacritics inline, \"dog\\t@U.F.A@dog+N+Sg\" where C++ prints \"dog\\tdog+N+Sg\". Cause: upstream's own alphabet reader rewrites each flag symbol's key-table entry to \"\" as it parses it (cc:463, kt->operator[](k) = strdup(\"\")), so flags can never reach an analysis; the library's OL alphabet is constructed with preserve_diacritic_strings = true (transducer.rs:2591) because other callers need the strings, and the tool concatenated the path as-is. Fixed by filtering flag symbols out of the path in analyze(), the same display-layer filter lookup.rs:612 and flookup.rs:558 already apply. AFTER THE FIX: 126 of 126 byte-identical.\n\nREAL-WORLD A/B. smj-tts-pipe/analyser-gt-norm.hfstol, 28MB, 538843 states, 76 flag symbols in the alphabet, against 16922 word forms harvested from lang-smj's morphology lexc. -u and -u -w: byte-identical, 56098 lines each. Plain and -w: the multiset of output lines is exactly equal (122214 lines, sort | cmp) but intra-record ORDER differs among equal-weight analyses.\n\nThat last one is a real divergence and it is NOT this node's. It is flag-independent — reproduced on a flag-free three-analysis regex where C++ -w prints dog+V+Inf, dog+N+Sg, dog+A+Cmp in traversal order and we print them lexicographically. C++ TransducerW::printAnalyses walks a std::multimap<Weight,string>, which preserves insertion order among equivalent keys; our Variant::WPlain sorts a Vec built from the engine's HfstOneLevelPaths, a BTreeSet keyed on path content, so traversal order is already gone before the display sink sees anything. Fixing it means changing the library's path container, which is nowhere near this node — filed here for whoever picks it up. Only the weighted non-unique variant is affected; -u sorts by string in both implementations and agrees exactly.\n\nACTION. Variant::Fd, Variant::FdUniq, Variant::WFd, Variant::WFdUniq deleted (four, not three — FdUniq was unreachable too, it just shared an arm so nothing flagged it), the duplicate WFd arm in print_analyses deleted, and the #[allow(dead_code)] removed outright rather than narrowed. Comments at the enum and at the selection site record why the third selector is absent. hfst-optimized-lookup.setup-fn carries a PORT DIVERGENCE note for the eight-to-four collapse and for the flag-filtering the port has to do by hand. New regression test flag_diacritics_filter_the_traversal_without_reaching_the_output covers both formats: no flag reaches the output, and a flag-blocked path still comes back +?.\n\nThe blank-line punctuation from optimized_lookup_smoke.rs is untouched and verified intact — the flag fixtures reproduce both shapes (one blank line for the weighted +? record, three for the unweighted and for tokenization failure) against C++ exactly.\n\nVerification: cargo nextest run 425 passed / 4 skipped / 0 failed (baseline 424, +1 the new test); cargo clippy --all-targets 0 warnings 0 errors; cargo fmt --check clean; cargo check --no-default-features clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:34:33.343572Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id xfst-diagnostics
        title "Thread ariadne spans through the xfst compiler: it has ~59 raw error!/eprintln! sites and zero crate::diag usage, while xre, twolc, lexc and pmatch already render source-anchored snippets. xfst is the interactive/script surface where a user reads errors most, and an unanchored \"could not be parsed\" naming neither line nor file is the worst diagnostic in the tree — the `set copyright-owner \"Acme Corp\"` rejection this session gave no hint that the quoting, not the command, was the problem. Carry the span from nfst-xfst's Spanned AST into the compiler's error paths and route them through diag::emit. Per [dec:hfst:independent-fork], diagnostic quality is in scope on its own merits"
        priority 1
        kind @feat
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-12T09:21:27.71146Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Landed on nplan/xfst-diagnostics (51937a4), merged as 99eaf07. Verified here: `set copyright-owner \"Acme Corp\"` now renders a caret at 1:21 under the quote with two notes — \"xfst has no quoted strings: a word ends at the first space or quote\" and \"escape each special character with '%', e.g. 'Acme% Corp' for the value 'Acme Corp'\". That is the exact question I had to read the C++ flex lexer to answer earlier in the session. A mistyped `detrminize` at line 6 of 8 reports 6:1 with \"did you mean 'determinize'?\".\n\n28 of 33 error! and all 30 warn! sites are on the diag path. Leverage is wider than the count: converting top() (37 callers) and error_message() (9 callers) anchored ~40 report-only sites off ONE assignment of current_span per top-level command in parse().\n\nThe 5 left alone are correct to leave: internal invariants with no user span (no-colon-in-line, two FATAL could-not-return-to-level, test_uni's self-passed argument). A caret on those would point at whatever command happened to be running — the brief said a wrong caret is worse than none, and it held to that.\n\nI made the one-line hookup the agent flagged as outside its ownership: comp.set_source_name(filename) in hfst-cli/src/tools/xfst.rs parse_file. Headers now read \"/tmp/bad.xfst:6:1\" instead of \"<xfst>:6:1\", so the location is clickable.\n\nTwo design points worth keeping. Regex bodies: nfst-xfst parses them as standalone strings so inner spans count from the body; regex_body_diagnostics re-parses blank-padded to the true offset (newlines kept, other bytes spaced) and rebases, on the error path only. And compile_spanned_xre now clears xre.source before evaluating an embedded body, fixing a latent bug where a stale source from an earlier xre.compile would have had body-relative spans read against it.\n\nDeliberately BACKED OUT by the agent, and right to: rebasing spans for eval-time xre diagnostics cost a re-parse per regex (25% on a 62 KB/1500-define script, needing a work budget to bound the quadratic) and it could not trigger a single reachable xre eval-time diagnostic through xfst — is_valid_function_call and warn_about_xfst_special_symbol are dead from that path, the rest are verbose-gated. Perf is at baseline (2.55s vs 2.54s on a 634 KB/15000-define script)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-12T09:21:47.266775Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }
